{
  "evidence": "The text lists Labour’s 1997 ‘five pledges’ (class sizes, halve time to sentence persistent young offenders, cut NHS waiting lists via £100m from bureaucracy, a windfall levy to get 250,000 under‑25s into work, and no rise in basic/top income tax with VAT on fuel cut to 5%); promises Bank of England reform; creation of a Scottish Parliament, Welsh Assembly, and a London mayor; a national minimum wage set by an independent Low Pay Commission; ending hereditary peers’ voting rights; a free vote on a complete ban on handguns; establishment of a Food Standards Agency, NESTA, the University for Industry and Individual Learning Accounts; a Freedom of Information Act; and the stance of ‘no case for new nuclear power stations’ alongside a referendum on joining the single currency.",
  "confidence": "high",
  "identification": "A summary of the UK Labour Party’s 1997 general election manifesto, ‘New Labour: Because Britain Deserves Better,’ including its five-pledge card; published by the Labour Party (April 1997, United Kingdom)."
}
